# Codex provider-session access probe

This is a one-shot, bounded feasibility probe for the Context Magnet ETL. It begins with a completed BB child thread's public event log, accepts `providerThreadId` only after an event proves `providerId: codex`, and records the event high-watermark plus a terminal `providerCheckpointId` only when that terminal event emits one.

The preferred target is `thr_c9xdr46tjs`; the runner rejects itself as target. The live route must already own that provider thread through exactly one enabled subscription-router account. It cannot assign, repair, migrate, copy, resume, or backfill. The routed proxy is prohibited. The normal router status command is also excluded: opening its store may alter permissions or reap a lease, so it is not a read-only authority. The probe must fail closed when a safe server-filtered identity projection is unavailable; it never fetches a general event log and filters it locally.

An external, already-read-only owner attestation is the sole admission input. It must bind one enabled owner, exact provider thread, route revision, attested realCodex executable, and account home. The runner launches the direct executable against that home, sends `initialize`, `initialized`, then `thread/read(includeTurns:true)`, and rejects server-originated requests. A changed revision, ambiguous owner, wrong provider, unknown request, unsupported native shape, timeout, or any limit breach fails closed.

The direct response is sensitive. Stdout before newline buffering is byte-capped, and stderr and a single line are capped independently. The probe allows one child process group at a time, has wall-time, turn, item, and fragment ceilings, and always signals then reaps its process group. It decodes only the vendored 0.153.2 receipt shapes and emits only identity metadata: BB thread, Codex `Thread.id`, Codex `Thread.sessionId`, turn/item identifiers, fragment ordinal, hook-run identifier, byte length, and SHA-256 digest. `Thread.id` and `sessionId` are independently bound and never equated.

The fixture covers the successful routed shape and native, absent, archived, disabled, divergent, unauthorized, forked, compacted, interrupted, failed, subagent, zero-work, unknown-request, every bound breach, timeout, teardown, revision-race, and ambiguous-owner failures. A live unavailable result is valid only when the authoritative non-mutating route is absent, as on this host: the router's own status opening attempted a permission change and was rejected. No fragment text, messages, reasoning, commands, tool data, credentials, account-home value, raw response, rollout path, or crash body is emitted or persisted.

Before and after a successful direct read, the caller must compare BB high-watermark, status/checkpoint, router revision and ownership, safe rollout identity metadata, hook receipt metadata, process inventory, and workspace/runtime hashes. Any difference is mutation evidence, not success. The supplied runner cannot claim that comparison while route admission is unavailable; it reports a bounded blocker instead.
